The following hold for the edge law and the Gaussian finite models.*

1.  *Let $b+g_1>0$, $\ell_a=(g_a+b)^{-1}$. Almost surely $\sum_a\ell_a=\infty$ and $\sum_a\ell_a^2<\infty$. If $Z_a$ are independent $N(0,\ell_a)$, the densities $q$ of $\sum_a(Z_a^2-\ell_a)$ and $q_m$ of $\sum_{a>m}(Z_a^2-\ell_a)$ are continuous, bounded, and strictly positive. The first $m$ coordinates of $\Pi_g$ have density $$\label{eq:edge-cylinder-density}
           \frac{q_m\!\left(D_g(b)-\sum_{a\le m}(x_a^2-\ell_a)\right)}
                {q(D_g(b))}
           \prod_{a\le m}
              \frac{e^{-x_a^2/(2\ell_a)}}{\sqrt{2\pi\ell_a}}.$$ These consistent densities are measurable in $g$ and independent of $b$. For each fixed $0\le j<\infty$, $\Pi_g[|x_a|^j]\le C_{j,g,b}\ell_a^{j/2}$, with a finite constant uniform in $a$. For sufficiently large fixed $m$, the tail beyond $m$ has a density bounded by a finite constant relative to $\bigotimes_{a>m}N(0,\ell_a)$. Conditional on that tail, the first $m$ coordinates have the Gaussian angular law on the sphere of squared radius $$D_g(b)+\sum_{a\le m}\ell_a-\sum_{a>m}(x_a^2-\ell_a).$$ In particular the renormalized constraint holds $\Pi_g$-almost surely. These are the statements of (OpenAI 2026d, Proposition 2.3) [[FUA](#ref-FUA)].*

2.  *With $b_n=1+(-g_{1,n})_+$ and $b=1+(-g_1)_+$, the spherical and cube edge marginals converge jointly with the edge points to $\Pi_g$ on the represented subsequences described below. Cube integrals converge for every continuous cylinder of polynomial growth. Spherical coordinates obey the uniform bounds $$\mu^{\mathrm{sp}}[|x_a|^j]\le C_j(g_{a,n}+b_n)^{-j/2},
           \qquad
           \lim_{m\to\infty}\sup_n\sum_{a>m}(g_{a,n}+b_n)^{-2}=0$$ after discarding finitely many $n$ on such a representation. Here $\mu^{\mathrm{sp}}$ is surface measure on $\{|\sigma|^2=n\}$, tilted by $\exp(\sigma^{\mathsf T}W\sigma/2)$. The sources are (OpenAI 2026d, Proposition 2.3 and Corollary 3.3) [[FUA](#ref-FUA)].*

3.  *The Euclidean gradient on smooth compact cylinders is closable from $L^2(\Pi_g)$ to $L^2(\Pi_g;\ell^2)$. The associated closed form has kernel equal to the constants. Every coordinate belongs to its domain and satisfies $\nabla x_a=e_a$, hence $\mathcal E_g(x_a)=1$. This is (OpenAI 2026d, Proposition 5.1) [[FUA](#ref-FUA)]. The usual contraction rule for gradients passes to the closure, so the form is Dirichlet and $S_t^g$ is a conservative symmetric Markov semigroup.*


4.  *On a quenched represented subsequence, say that $f_n$ converges weakly to $F\in L^2(\Pi_g)$ if its $L^2(\mu)$ norms are bounded and $\mu[f_n\phi(x)]\to\Pi_g[F\phi]$ for every smooth compact cylinder $\phi$; call the convergence strong when in addition $\mu[f_n^2]\to\Pi_g[F^2]$. Suppose, for fixed $C,D<\infty$, that $\|f_n\|_\infty\le Cn^D$, that $T_n\mathcal E(f_n)$ is bounded, and that $f_n$ converges weakly to $F$. Then $F$ belongs to the closed form domain and $$\label{eq:relaxed-liminf}
           \liminf_n T_n\mathcal E(f_n)\ge c_*\mathcal E_g(F).$$ Conversely, for every smooth compact cylinder $F$, there are functions $f_{n,j}$, polynomially bounded for each fixed $j$, such that $f_{n,j}$ converges strongly to $F$ as $n\to\infty$ for each $j$, and $$\label{eq:relaxed-recovery}
           \lim_{j\to\infty}\lim_{n\to\infty}T_n\mathcal E(f_{n,j})
              =c_*\mathcal E_g(F).$$ The source asserts a common represented event for countably many chosen sources and tests. Recovery is used at each fixed $j$, followed by the displayed $j$-limit (OpenAI 2026d, Theorem 6.8) [[FUA](#ref-FUA)].

Fix $0<C_1<\infty$, $0\le R<\infty$, positive tolerances $\epsilon_{\rm sp},\epsilon_0,\epsilon_v,\epsilon_g$, and $\zeta>0$. For every $\varepsilon>0$ there are finite positive constants $K,C,c,a,v_0$, a width $s_0>0$, a radius $R_{\rm ann}<\infty$, and Gaussian disorder events $\mathcal C_n$ with $\liminf_n\mathbb P_{\rm GOE}(\mathcal C_n)\ge1-\varepsilon$, such that the following hold on $\mathcal C_n$, simultaneously at every scale $$\label{edge:scale-range}
 K\le k_p=np^3,\qquad p\le C_1r_n.$$*


1.  *The active dimension is between $ck_p$ and $Ck_p$, $d_1\ge-\epsilon_{\rm sp}p^2$, and, with $b_0=4/(3\pi)$, $$\label{edge:trace-estimates}
     \left|\frac{n-\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}R_{p,0}}{np}-b_0\right|\le\epsilon_{\rm sp},
     \qquad
     \left|\frac p n\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}R_{p,0}^2-2b_0\right|\le\epsilon_{\rm sp}.$$ On $\|h\|\le Rp^2M_p$, the saddle satisfies $-1+c_R\le\alpha_p(h)/p^2\le C_R$. On $|u|\le s_0p^2$, $\ell_p(u)\asymp p^2M_p$ and $\ell'_p(u)\asymp M_p$.*


2.  *On the field ball $\|h\|\le Rp^2M_p$, $$\label{edge:static-estimates}
     \begin{split}
     K_p(h)&\preceq C_Rp^{-2}I_{\mathcal H_p},\\
     |\phi_p(h)-\phi_p^{\rm sp}(h)|&\le\epsilon_v k_p,\\
     \|P_pm_p(h)-R_{p,\alpha_p(h)}h\|&\le\epsilon_gM_p .
     \end{split}$$ The constant $C_R$ in the first line may be chosen independently of the requested annular tolerance $\epsilon_0$. On the full annulus $|\alpha_p(h)|\le s_0p^2$, the field is nonzero; for $e=h/\|h\|$, $$\label{edge:coarse-curvature}
     K_p(h)\preceq(1+\epsilon_0)p^{-2}I_{\mathcal H_p},
     \qquad p^2\langle e,K_p(h)e\rangle\le0.9 .$$ The annulus lies in $\|h\|\le R_{\rm ann}p^2M_p$, and the event also includes the static comparisons on this ball, with constants allowed to depend on $R_{\rm ann}$.*



3.  *For every $v\ge v_0$, the uncapped law $\mu$, every posterior $\mu_{p,h}$ in the stated field ball, and their spherical counterparts satisfy $$\label{edge:projection-tails}
     \Pr\{\|P_pX\|>vM_p\}\le C e^{-c k_pv^6}.$$ The event is empty for $v>p^{-1/2}$. The spherical pair bound of Lemma <a href="#edge:spherical-tails" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="edge:spherical-tails">6</a> also holds for each number of replicas fixed in advance, with constants allowed to depend on that number. Moreover, $$\label{edge:ordinary-annulus}
     Q_\mu\{|\alpha_p(h_p)|>\zeta p^2\}\le C e^{-c k_p}.$$*


4.  *For every scale in <a href="#edge:scale-range" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="edge:scale-range">[edge:scale-range]</a> with $p\le r_n$, simultaneously for every $0\le f\le1$, $$\label{edge:variance-estimates}
     \begin{split}
     V_p(f)&\le Cp^{-2}\mathcal E(f)+C e^{-c k_p^a},\\
     p^2Q_\mu\|\nabla_{\mathcal H_p}F_p(h_p)\|^2
           &\le C V_p(f)+C e^{-c k_p^a}.
     \end{split}$$ The event and constants in these inequalities are independent of $f$.*

*All constants in the saddle, length, and comparison bounds are deterministic on $\mathcal C_n$ and may depend on the fixed inputs and the requested failure probability, but not on $n$, $p$, or the field in its stated region.

In the convention <a href="#edge:threshold-convention" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="edge:threshold-convention">[edge:threshold-convention]</a>, the measures $\nu_p=k_p^{-1}\sum_i\delta_{d_i/p^2}$ converge on fixed bounded intervals to $\pi^{-1}\sqrt v\,\mathbf 1_{\{v>0\}}\mathrm dv$. Their distribution functions converge uniformly on compact intervals. The same convention gives $d_1/p^2\ge-o(1)$, $\dim\mathcal H_p/k_p\to2/(3\pi)$, and the two limits in <a href="#edge:trace-estimates" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="edge:trace-estimates">[edge:trace-estimates]</a>. On spectral events of arbitrarily large limiting probability there is also a fixed $C$ such that $$\label{edge:counting-tail}
 \begin{aligned}
 \#\{i:d_i\le u\}&\le Cnu^{3/2}
       &&(u\ge cp^2,\ u\le4),\\
 \int_{[A,\infty)}(1+v)^{-2}\nu_p(\mathrm dv)&\le CA^{-1/2}
       &&(A\ge1),
 \end{aligned}$$ for every fixed $c>0$, after increasing the threshold. Every spectral restriction below also includes eigenvalue-only events $\mathcal S_n^{\rm glob}$ of probability $1-o(1)$ on which, uniformly over their spectra, $$\label{edge:global-spectral}
 \frac1n\sum_i\delta_{\lambda_i}\ \Longrightarrow\
 \rho_{\rm sc}(\,\mathrm dx):=\frac1{2\pi}\sqrt{4-x^2}\,
             \mathbf 1_{\{|x|\le2\}}\,\mathrm dx,\qquad
 \lambda_1\longrightarrow2,\qquad \|W\|\le3 .$$

Fix a field radius and a fixed number of replicas. On the spectral restrictions above, for every $p$ with $k_p\ge K$ and $p\le C_1r_n$, both the uncapped zero-field spherical law and the capped spherical law at a field in that ball satisfy $$\label{edge:spherical-tail-bounds}
 \begin{aligned}
 \Pr\{\|P_pX\|>vM_p\}&\le C e^{-c k_pv^6}
       &&(v\ge v_0),\\
 \Pr\{|n^{-1}X^{\mathsf T}X'|>w\}&\le C e^{-c n w^3}
       &&(Cp\le w\le\rho_0).
 \end{aligned}$$ Here $X,X'$ are independent replicas at the same field, $\rho_0>0$ is fixed and small, and the second bound holds for every pair of a fixed finite family. The constants are uniform in $p,n$.

For GOE-almost every $g$, and every $b$ with $b+g_1>0$, there are finite positive constants $c_{g,b},C_{g,b}$ such that $$\label{edge:limit-rate-bounds}
 c_{g,b}(1+i)^{2/3}\le g_i+b\le C_{g,b}(1+i)^{2/3}
 \qquad(i\ge1).$$ They can be chosen measurably when $b$ is a measurable admissible function of $g$.

Let $\mathcal{R}_n=\{-1+2j/n:0\le j\le n\}$ and $I_r=[r-1/n,r+1/n]$. The overlap of two independent uniform cube spins has mass $$p_n(r)=2^{-n}\binom{n}{n(1+r)/2},\qquad r\in\mathcal{R}_n,$$ whereas the overlap of two independent uniform spherical spins has density $$f_n(s)=\frac{\Gamma(n/2)}{\sqrt\pi\,\Gamma((n-1)/2)}
             (1-s^2)^{(n-3)/2},\qquad -1<s<1.$$ For a sufficiently small fixed $\rho_0>0$, uniformly for large $n$ and $r\in\mathcal{R}_n$ with $|r|\le\rho_0$, $$\label{edge:pair-cell-mass}
 p_n(r)\le e^{C+Cnr^4}\int_{I_r}f_n(s)\,\mathrm ds.$$ Let $H_2$ be the uniform law of an ordered orthonormal pair $(q,q')$. If a positive measurable angular weight $a_n(s,q,q')$ has finite integrals and satisfies, for $H_2$-almost every frame, $$|\log a_n(s,q,q')-\log a_n(r,q,q')|\le K_0
 \quad\text{for almost every }s\in I_r,$$ then $$\label{edge:pair-cell-weighted}
 p_n(r)\int a_n(r,q,q')\,\mathrm dH_2
 \le e^{C+Cnr^4+K_0}
       \int_{I_r}\!\int a_n(s,q,q')\,\mathrm dH_2\,f_n(s)\,\mathrm ds.$$ The cells have disjoint interiors. Summing over their midpoints in an overlap interval enlarges the spherical interval by at most $1/n$ at each endpoint.

On the fixed spectral restrictions, there are $a_{\rm an},c,C>0$ such that for every dyadic interval $[q,2q]$ with fixed buffers inside the enlarged scale range, $$\label{edge:shrinking-annulus}
 \mathbf P\left\{\exists p\in[q,2q]:
       |\alpha_p(h_p)|>Ck_p^{-1/10}p^2\right\}
       \le C e^{-c k_q^{a_{\rm an}}}.$$ For every fixed $\zeta>0$, after increasing the threshold, $$\label{edge:fixed-annulus-biased}
 \mathbf P\left\{\exists p\in[q,2q]:
       |\alpha_p(h_p)|>\zeta p^2\right\}
       \le C_\zeta e^{-c_\zeta k_q}.$$ Consequently <a href="#edge:ordinary-annulus" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="edge:ordinary-annulus">[edge:ordinary-annulus]</a> holds simultaneously at every scale on ordinary disorder events of arbitrarily large limiting probability.

Let $I=[q,2q]$, with a prescribed fixed multiplicative enlargement inside the scale range. At path fields write $$a_p=P_p(m_p-m_p^{\rm sp}),\qquad D_p=p^2K_p-I_{\mathcal H_p}.$$ There are $c,C,a_1>0$ such that $$\label{edge:score-bound}
 \mathbf P\left\{
   \int_I\|a_p\|^2\,\mathrm d(p^2)>Ck_q^{1/2}\right\}
       \le C e^{-c k_q^{a_1}},$$ and $$\label{edge:frobenius-bound}
 \mathbf P\left(
 \mathcal G\cap
 \left\{\int_q^{2q}\frac{\|D_p\|_{\rm F}^2}{k_p}\,\mathrm d\log p
              >Ck_q^{-1/5}\right\}\right)
       \le C e^{-c k_q^{a_1}} .$$ The same statements hold for every fixed enlargement of $I$, with adjusted constants. They are probability statements; no expectation of the untruncated score on their failures is asserted.

Fix $\beta>0$, $A>0$, $0<C_\star<\infty$, and $\vartheta>0$. On Gaussian disorder events of limiting lower probability at least $1-\vartheta$, with a fixed constant $C$, $$\sup_{\sigma}\ \sup_{1\le d\le C_\star T_n}
 \sum_\tau P_d(\sigma,\tau)
 \mathbf 1_{\left\{\log p_d(\sigma,\tau)>
       \frac12 n^\beta(T_n/d)^{3/2}\right\}}
 \le Cn^{-A}                                                \label{warm:kernel-tail}$$ for all sufficiently large $n$. For any initial law $\rho$, averaging the displayed row probabilities over $\sigma\sim\rho$ preserves the same bound.

For every $g$ as above, there is at most one family $(h_s)_{s>0}$ of probability densities with respect to $\Pi_g$ with the following properties:*

1.  *$h_s\ge0$, $\Pi_g[h_s]=1$, and $\sup_{s_0\le s\le s_1}\|h_s\|_{L^\infty(\Pi_g)}<\infty$ whenever $0<s_0<s_1<\infty$;*

2.  *$h_{s+t}=S_t^g h_s$ in $L^2(\Pi_g)$ for $s,t>0$, where $S_t^g=\exp(-t c_*H_g)$;*


3.  *there is a sequence $s_k\downarrow0$ such that, for every $i\ge1$ and $\varepsilon>0$, $$\label{entr:escape}
     \Pi_g\left[h_{s_k}\,
           \mathbf 1_{\{x_i^2/M(x)>\varepsilon\}}\right]\longrightarrow0.$$*


*Any such family satisfies $$\label{entr:zero-boundary}
 h_s\Pi_g\ \Longrightarrow\ \delta_{\boldsymbol 0}
 \quad\text{on \(\mathbb R^{\mathbb N}\) with its product topology, as \(s\downarrow0\)}.$$ For almost every $g$ such a family exists. It can be chosen measurably in $g$, as a family of $L^2(\Pi_g)$ density classes, and with jointly measurable density representatives in $(g,s,x)$. Every represented Gaussian limit in Proposition <a href="#prop:gaussian-subsequence" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:gaussian-subsequence">25</a> is this family almost surely.

For $L$ defined above, let $$\widehat K(z)=\frac{1}{D+z\widehat L(z)}\quad(z>0),$$ where a hat denotes the Laplace transform and every kernel is zero on negative times. The function $L$ is locally integrable, and $\widehat K$ is the Laplace transform of a nonnegative, decreasing function $K$, smooth on $(0,\infty)$. For $0<t\le1$, $$\label{entr:K-bounds}
 c_g t^{-1/2}\le K(t)\le C_g t^{-1/2},
 \qquad |K'(t)|\le C_g t^{-3/2}.$$ Set $K_i=K*E_i$. Then $$\begin{aligned}
 K_i(t)&\le C_g\frac{\sqrt t}{1+r_it},&
 |K_i'(t)|&\le C_g\frac{t^{-1/2}}{1+r_it},             \label{entr:Ki-bounds}\\
 2r_iK_i(t)&\longrightarrow K(t)                       \label{entr:Ki-limit}
\end{aligned}$$ uniformly on each compact subinterval of $(0,1]$ as $i\to\infty$. For $t>0$, $h\ge0$, $t+h\le1$, and $0\le\xi<1/4$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \sup_i r_iK_i(t)^2&\le C_g,&
 \sup_i r_i|K_i(t+h)-K_i(t)|^2
   &\le C_g\min\{1,h^2/t^2\},                          \label{entr:Ki-sup}\\
 \sum_i(1+r_i)^{2\xi}K_i(t)^2
   &\le C_{g,\xi}t^{-1/2-2\xi},                       \label{entr:Ki-sum}\\
 \sum_i|K_i(t+h)-K_i(t)|^2
   &\le C_g t^{-1/2}\min\{1,h^2/t^2\}.                \label{entr:Ki-increment}
\end{aligned}$$ As causal distributions, $$\label{entr:kernel-inverse}
 K*(D\delta_0+L')=\delta_0.$$

Let $(W_i)$ be independent Brownian motions in a filtration, let $(y_i)$ be measurable at time zero with $\sum_i y_i^2/(2r_i)=1$, and let $q$ be progressively measurable with $|q(v)|\le C_0v^{-1/4}$ for $0<v\le1$. Define $$\begin{aligned}
 Z_i^q(t)&=\sqrt2\int_0^t e^{-r_i(t-v)}q(v)\,dW_i(v),\notag\\
 \mathsf G_q^y(t)&=2\sqrt2\sum_i\int_0^t
                K_i(t-v)q(v)e^{-r_iv}y_i\,dW_i(v),\label{entr:stochastic-parts}\\
 \mathsf N_q(t)&=2\sqrt2\sum_i\int_0^t
                K_i(t-v)q(v)Z_i^q(v)\,dW_i(v).\notag
\end{aligned}$$ The two series converge in probability in $C([0,1])$, with value zero at zero. For every finite $j\ge2$, their partial sums and their limits satisfy $$\begin{aligned}
 \|Z_i^q(t)\|_{L^j}^2
     &\le C_{g,C_0,j}\frac{\sqrt t}{1+r_it},           \label{entr:Z-moment}\\
 \|\mathsf G_q^y(t)\|_{L^j}+\|\mathsf N_q(t)\|_{L^j}
     &\le C_{g,C_0,j}t^{1/4},                         \label{entr:stoch-moment}\\
 \|\mathsf G_q^y(t+h)-\mathsf G_q^y(t)\|_{L^j}
  +\|\mathsf N_q(t+h)-\mathsf N_q(t)\|_{L^j}
     &\le C_{g,C_0,j}h^{1/4}\quad(0\le t<t+h\le1).
                                                               \label{entr:stoch-increment}
\end{aligned}$$ The supremum on $[0,1]$ of either limit has every finite moment, bounded by a constant depending only on $g,C_0$, and that order.

Let the processes be as in <a href="#entr:YQ" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="entr:YQ">[entr:YQ]</a>–<a href="#entr:common-sde" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="entr:common-sde">[entr:common-sde]</a>, and let $A_y$ be the candidate seed response defined above. Almost surely, for $0<t\le1$, $$\label{entr:volterra-equation}
 f(t)=A_y(t)+2\sqrt2\sum_i\int_0^t
               K_i(t-v)Q(v)Y_i(v)\,dW_i(v).$$ The stochastic series is defined by localization and has a continuous version on every compact subinterval of $(0,1]$.

Suppose that, along a sequence of initial states possibly random, $\sum_i p_i=1$ and $p_i\to0$ in probability for every fixed $i$. Then $$\label{entr:A-limit}
 A_y\longrightarrow K
 \quad\text{in probability, uniformly on compact subintervals of \((0,1]\)}.$$ For each fixed $t>0$, the seed stochastic term $\mathsf G_q^y(t)$ tends to zero in $L^2$, uniformly over all adapted $q$ satisfying $|q(v)|\le C_0v^{-1/4}$.

There is a smooth function $\chi_n(\theta)\in[0,1]$ with the following properties. On its support, and on the support of any of its derivatives of order at most $K$, the matrix satisfies $$\label{hist:static-conditions}
 \begin{gathered}
 \mu^{\otimes2}(A_2(d))\le e^{-a_2(d)},\qquad
 \mu^{\otimes4}(A_4(d))\le e^{-a_4(d)},\qquad d\in\mathcal{D}_n,\\
 n^{-6}\sum_{i<j}\theta_{ij}^2\le2.
 \end{gathered}$$ An assertion involving an omitted event is void. For some $b=b(\beta)>0$, every derivative of $\chi_n$ of positive order at most $K$ in one coefficient $\theta_e$, and every differentiated factor in its product construction, is $O(n^{-b})$, uniformly in $e$ and $\theta$. For Gaussian couplings, $\chi_n=1$ with probability tending to one.

For every level in <a href="#hist:node-level" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="hist:node-level">[hist:node-level]</a> there is a smooth $W_N:\mathbb R\to(0,1)$ such that $$\label{hist:cap-and-plateau}
 e^lW_N(l)\le e^{CN},\qquad
 1-W_N(l)\le e^{-(\log n)^2/2}\quad(l\le N/2).$$ For $1\le k\le K$ its derivative in $l$ satisfies globally $$\label{hist:global-ratios}
 \frac{|W_N^{(k)}|}{W_N}+\frac{|W_N^{(k)}|}{1-W_N}\le C_K,
 \qquad
 \frac{|W_N^{(k)}|}{1-W_N}\le\frac{C_K(\log n)^{2K}}N.$$ For each fixed $L$, wherever $W_N(l)\ge n^{-L}$ one also has $$\label{hist:survival-hazard}
 \frac{|W_N^{(k)}(l)|}{W_N(l)}
 \le \frac{C_{K,L}(\log n)^{2K}}N\bigl(1-W_N(l)\bigr).$$ Moreover, uniformly over these levels and for sufficiently large $n$, $$\label{hist:log-cap-growth}
 |\log W_N(l)|+|\log(1-W_N(l))|
       \le C\bigl(1+N+|l|+(\log n)^2\bigr).$$

Let $I$ be a fixed node of a checked root in a tested path law. Given the marked history outside the interior of $I$ and its two boundary states, the conditional law inside $I$ is $\mathsf C_I^{X_a,X_b}$. In particular, resampling this interior independently from that law preserves the tested marginal. For an internal node its midpoint $z$ under $\mathsf C_I^{x,y}$ has probability $$\label{hist:midpoint-probability}
 \pi_I^{x,y}(z)=
   \frac{\mu(z)k_{I^-}(x,z)k_{I^+}(z,y)}{\bar k_I(x,y)},$$ and conditional on $z$ the two interiors are independent with laws $\mathsf C_{I^-}^{x,z}$ and $\mathsf C_{I^+}^{z,y}$.*


*For every $A$, there is $L=L(A,M_+,M_-)<\infty$ such that $\bar k_I(X_a,X_b)\ge n^{-L}$ outside an endpoint event of probability $n^{-A}$ under any such tested law. If $d=|I|$ exceeds a fixed constant, then for these boundaries the density of its first two thirds under $\mathsf C_I^{x,y}$ relative to the ordinary unconditioned prefix from $x$ is at most $$\label{hist:prefix-density}
 n^L e^{C'N(d)}.$$ The reversed last two thirds have the same bound relative to the ordinary prefix from $y$ in reversed time.

Let $\mathcal F_t$ be the natural filtration of a nonexplosive marked path, augmented by an initial $\sigma$-field, and let $\mathsf Q\ll\mathsf P$ be a change of law up to a finite stopping time $\tau$, with density $Z$ there and with intensities $rb$ relative to the reference intensities $r$ conditional on that initial information. For the cost in <a href="#score:hellinger-cost" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="score:hellinger-cost">[score:hellinger-cost]</a>, $$\label{score:hellinger-inequality}
 \mathsf Q\{\mathcal J_{[0,\tau]}>A,\ \log Z\le B\}
          \le e^{(B-A)/2}.$$ Consequently, for every fixed $\eta>0$, with arbitrarily high inverse-polynomial probability, $$\label{score:action-bounds}
 \begin{cases}
 \mathcal J_{\rm path}\le n^\eta,&\text{under a tested forward law},\\
 \mathcal J_{\rm portion}\le C N(|I|)+n^\eta,
       &\text{under either oriented portion of }\mathsf C_I^{x,y},\quad |I|\ge d_0.
 \end{cases}$$ Here $d_0$ is a fixed constant large enough for Equation <a href="#hist:prefix-density" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="hist:prefix-density">[hist:prefix-density]</a>. The second assertion is over the tested boundary law followed by the conditional interior. In all these cases the integrated new intensity at any one site on each specified unit-length block is at most $n^\eta$ with the same probability convention. These integrated intensities and $\mathcal J$ have polynomial fixed-order moments, also after averaging over the boundaries of a conditional interior.

For every fixed $1\le p<\infty$ and $1\le l\le K$, under ordinary forward dynamics on an interval of length $d\ge1$, $$\label{score:martingale-moment}
 \left\|\sup_{t\in I}|\ell_{l,e}([\inf I,t])|\right\|_{L^p}
       \le C_{p,K}d^{l/2}.$$ For every marked path the exact likelihood reversal is $$\label{score:reversal}
 \frac{L_{I,e}^{\to}(u)}{1+u q_e(X_{\sup I})}
   =\frac{L_{I,e}^{\leftarrow}(u)}{1+u q_e(X_{\inf I})}.$$ It follows that, for every fixed $\eta>0$, with arbitrarily high inverse-polynomial probability under tested laws and their resampled marginals, simultaneously over entries and specified tree intervals, $$\label{score:raw-high-probability}
 |\ell_{l,e}^{\to}(I)|+|\ell_{l,e}^{\leftarrow}(I)|
       \le n^\eta |I|^{l/2},\qquad 1\le l\le K.$$ On a unit-length interval this also holds for partial products, with an arbitrarily small power allowance, when a bounded fixed-order product of earlier coefficients is included.

Fix an admissible oriented law $\mathsf Q$, its oriented filtration, and any permitted auxiliary-data augmentation. Fix a dyadic subtree $H$ of length $d$ in that oriented portion and an order $1\le l\le K$. There is a finite family of time-integral vectors $V_\alpha^{(l)}(H)$, chosen from this fixed setup without reference to a subsequent choice of $A$, such that for every vector $A_e$ measurable at the oriented start of $H$ for which the displayed products are integrable, coordinatewise, $$\label{score:raw-representation}
 \mathsf E_{\mathsf Q}[A_e\ell_{l,e}(H)]
       =\mathsf E_{\mathsf Q}\left[A_e\sum_\alpha V_{\alpha,e}^{(l)}(H)\right].$$ The vectors and $A$ may depend on the same initial information. With arbitrarily high inverse-polynomial probability, using the joint boundary-and-interior convention for a bridge portion, their sum of norms obeys $$\label{score:raw-projection-bounds}
 \begin{split}
 \sum_\alpha|V_\alpha^{(l)}(H)|_2
       &\lesssim_\beta d^{(l-1)/2+1/4}\sqrt{\mathcal J_H/n},\\
 \sum_\alpha|V_\alpha^{(l)}(H)|_4
       &\lesssim_\beta d^{(l-1)/2+5/16}n^{-1/4}\sqrt{\mathcal J_H}.
 \end{split}$$ Thus earlier vector factors can be included by Hölder’s inequality after the identity. This is a representation inside expectation, not a pointwise assertion about the raw coefficient $\ell_l(H)$.

*(i) Conditional coefficients.* For every deterministic matrix on <a href="#hist:static-conditions" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="hist:static-conditions">[hist:static-conditions]</a>, every tested path law, and every node $I$ of length $D$ in its checked roots, the vectors in <a href="#score:R-definition" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="score:R-definition">[score:R-definition]</a> satisfy, with arbitrarily high inverse-polynomial probability over their boundary states, $$\label{score:R-basic}
 |R_l(I)|_\infty\lesssim_\beta D^{l/2},\qquad
 |R_l(I)|_2\lesssim_\beta D^{l/2-1},\qquad
 |R_l(I)|_4\lesssim_\beta D^{l/2-1/2},\qquad 1\le l\le K.$$ Also, $$\label{score:R-l1}
 |R_3(I)|_1\lesssim_\beta D^{-1/4},\qquad
 |R_l(I)|_1\lesssim_\beta D^{(l-4)/2},\quad 4\le l\le K.$$ If $D\ge Tn^{-0.005}$, the $L^4$ bound for $R_1$ has the additional factor $n^{-0.01}$, the $L^2$ bounds for $R_2,R_3$ have the additional factor $n^{-0.001}$, and the $L^1$ bound for $R_4$ has the additional factor $n^{-0.0007}$. The conclusions hold simultaneously for any polynomial collection of specified nodes and any fixed number of conditional copies, with the same exceptional-set convention.*



**(ii) Raw representations.* Fix an admissible oriented law, its oriented filtration, and any permitted auxiliary-data augmentation, as in Lemma <a href="#score:raw-projection" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="score:raw-projection">44</a>. Then fix a dyadic subtree $H$ of length $d$ in that portion and an order $1\le l\le K$. The following representations satisfy <a href="#score:raw-representation" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="score:raw-representation">[score:raw-representation]</a> for every earlier vector $A$ in that lemma’s class, with the representation chosen from this fixed setup before $A$. If $l\ge2$ and $d\ge Tn^{-0.02}$, there is a representation for which $$\label{score:raw-improvement}
 \sum_\alpha|V_\alpha^{(l)}(H)|_2
       \lesssim_\beta n^{-0.004}
                   d^{(l-1)/2+1/4}\sqrt{\mathcal J_H/n}.$$ For every $1\le l\le K$, there is a representation for which $$\label{score:raw-l1-projection}
 \sum_\alpha|V_\alpha^{(l)}(H)|_1
       \lesssim_\beta \max\{\sqrt d,d^{l/2-5/4}\}\sqrt{\mathcal J_H/n}.$$ For $l=4$ and $d\ge Tn^{-0.006}$, the latter estimate has the additional factor $n^{-0.001}$. The two families here may differ from each other and from the family giving the two basic bounds in Lemma <a href="#score:raw-projection" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="score:raw-projection">44</a>. These pathwise norm estimates have arbitrarily high inverse-polynomial probability with that lemma’s scope: for a bridge portion the probability is under the tested boundary law followed by the conditional interior. They are not uniform conditional assertions at every fixed boundary pair.

There are constants $a>0$ and $C_K<\infty$ with the following property for all sufficiently small fixed $\beta>0$. Fix $A,B<\infty$, at most $K$ histories of the form <a href="#repl:tested-integral" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="repl:tested-integral">[repl:tested-integral]</a>, and integers $j_\alpha\ge0$ with $1\le k=\sum_\alpha j_\alpha\le K$. Suppose $0\le v_\alpha\le n^B$. On the deterministic static support of Lemma <a href="#hist:static-support" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="hist:static-support">33</a>, put $$\mathcal{D}_e=\prod_\alpha [u^{j_\alpha}]Z_{\alpha,e}(u).$$ Then, uniformly in the selected histories, $$\begin{aligned}
 |\mathcal{D}|_1
 &\le C\left(\prod_\alpha z_\alpha\right)
 \begin{cases}
 n^{-a+C_K\beta},&1\le k\le4,\\
 n^{C_K\beta}T^{(k-4)/2},&5\le k\le K,
 \end{cases}
 +n^{-A},                                                     \label{repl:averaged-history}\\
 |\mathcal{D}|_\infty
 &\le C n^{C_K\beta}T^{k/2}\prod_\alpha z_\alpha+n^{-A}.
                                                               \label{repl:sup-history}
\end{aligned}$$ The constant $C$ and the threshold for $n$ may depend on $A,B$ and on the fixed rectangles. The exponent constant $C_K$ is independent of these precision parameters and of $\beta$. The same estimates hold for specified derivatives in $\vartheta$ in place of the coefficients in $u$. Signed tests are covered by applying the statement to their positive and negative parts.
